{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean head 2520cffdcdf1bda5ccf617159136952227985090 / internal/gitstore artifact f42cd5c0.\n\nArchitecture: this changes layer 1, Ordinary Git storage (`internal/gitstore`), and no Workroom, kernel, application, service, or UI semantic boundary. Returning an error for an untrustworthy history row preserves the documented adapter contract that failed Git questions are distinct from negative answers, so docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update. The parser split is appropriately inside the storage adapter.\n\nSecurity: the ordinary 0x1e/0x1f forgery is closed and the focused tests pass, but the load-bearing NUL claim is too broad and the request's round-trip-or-fail-closed condition is not met. I created a raw commit with `git hash-object --literally` whose body contains NUL, updated a branch to it, and pushed it into a default bare repository; receive-pack accepted it. `git fsck --strict` then reports `nulInCommit`, proving the stored object is malformed, but the exact `git log -z` format used here emits eight valid-looking fields with subject and author intact and an empty body. Graph therefore succeeds while silently truncating attacker-controlled metadata: neither round-trip nor fail closed. Repair by detecting and refusing raw malformed commit objects (or by reading size-framed raw commit objects); add a regression that creates the `--literally` object, reaches it through a ref/default receive path, and proves Graph errors. Do not rely solely on porcelain commit creation. The review condition also names hostile decorations, but the suite has no real ref-name regression; pin that Git rejects the separator/control-byte ref or narrow the claim. The branch head is not yet present on origin, so push the repaired exact head before re-review.\n\nSimplification: keep the single fixed-stride parser and fail-closed field/timestamp guards; they are clear and mutation-sensitive. Reuse one repository fixture and one raw